Survivors, both `terminal 188x256`, both 772 B / 2698 B == C's byte count:

| cell | flip |
|---|---|
| `p2 q55` | one field: chroma `uv=5` + `aduv=-1` (C) vs `UV_DC_PRED` (port) at mi=(50,42) |
| `p4 q12` | one field: chroma `UV_CFL_PRED` (C) vs `UV_DC_PRED` (port) at mi=(46,46) |

## The controlled A/B — they are not alignment defects

The 2026-08-13 note said "the same content at the fully-aligned 192x256
byte-matches". That comparison used `crop:`, which CENTRE-CROPS: the 188-wide
and 192-wide runs sampled DIFFERENT source windows, so two things changed at
once and the result was not evidence about alignment.

The controlled version (`tools/pad_yuv.py`, added for this): take the frame the
188x256 encode actually sees AFTER its own TRUE->ALIGNED edge replication, and
feed it back as a genuine 192x256 frame via `raw:`. Identical pixels;
`true == aligned`; no padding, no straddle.

```
q55 p2: 188x256 DIFFERS | SAME PIXELS at 192x256 DIFFERS
q12 p4: 188x256 DIFFERS | SAME PIXELS at 192x256 DIFFERS
```

Same single flip, same mi, same op index (`p4 q12`: op 29127, `CDF14 C=s13
Rust=s0`, which is the op the 188-wide run reported too). **Alignment is
exonerated for both.**

The `crop:` breadth sweep says the same thing from the other side — the
divergence follows the CONTENT WINDOW, not the dimension:

| terminal crop | q55 p2 | q12 p4 |
|---|---|---|
| 128x128 | IDENTICAL | IDENTICAL |
| 192x192 | IDENTICAL | IDENTICAL |
| 192x256 | IDENTICAL | IDENTICAL |
| 256x160 | IDENTICAL | IDENTICAL |
| 256x256 | IDENTICAL | IDENTICAL |
| **320x256** | **DIFFERS** | **DIFFERS** |

## What they are, and what is ruled out

A CHROMA INTRA MODE divergence in the MDS3 chroma decision, one block per
frame, on real screen content. In `p2 q55` the LUMA symbols agree exactly
either side of it (op 19563 `CDF13 s=5` y_mode, op 19564 `CDF7 s=2` = luma
angle delta -1, both matching); only the `CDF14` uv_mode at op 19565 flips, and
C's uv pair (5, -1) is precisely its luma pair — uv-follows-luma — while the
port falls back to UV_DC.

**Ruled out: the ind-uv CANDIDATE SET.** The obvious suspect was that C sweeps
all uv modes x all 7 angle deltas where the port only tests the MDS3 survivors'
pairs. It does not: `search_best_mds3_uv_mode` (product_coding_loop.c:7301-7383
— the chroma_level-4 / `ind_uv_last_mds = 2` entry that preset 2 takes) builds
its candidate list from `best_candidate_index_array`'s `(uv_mode,
angle_delta[PLANE_TYPE_UV])` pairs, skipping inter/intrabc/UV_DC and
duplicates, then appends UV_DC — which is exactly what `leaf_funnel.rs`'s
`ind_uv_mds3` arm builds. The full mode x delta sweep at
product_coding_loop.c:7546-7589 belongs to the OTHER function,
`search_best_independent_uv_mode`, which is the chroma_level 1/2 (M0/M1) entry
and is not reached at preset 2. C's own per-candidate dump at the flipped block
(`SVT_UVLOOP_OUT`/`SVT_UVLOOP_XY="168,200"`, 157 calls) is consistent with that
reading: the uv=5 rows walk the LUMA candidates' deltas, interleaved with the
per-candidate CFL alpha search.

**Also ruled out: the pair never reaching the list.** `leaf_funnel.rs`'s
uv-follows-luma injection is `(uv_from_y(map_mode), delta)` — it carries the
LUMA angle delta, not 0 — so a surviving luma-D113/delta-(-1) candidate injects
exactly the `(5, -1)` pair C picks, and `ind_uv_mds3` puts every distinct
survivor pair into `uv_list`. The candidate is therefore tested and loses on
cost.

**NOT root-caused beyond that.** What is left is the chroma full-loop cost
itself (`chroma_eval` / the lambda it is compared under) or the MDS3 survivor
ORDER feeding the per-luma-mode argmin. Measuring it needs a per-candidate cost
dump inside `leaf_funnel.rs`, which a concurrent session owns; C's half is
already available (`SVT_UVLOOP_OUT` gives `cbb/crb/cbd/crd` per uv candidate).
Whether this is a genuine RD near-tie has NOT been established — no cost pair
has been compared, so the "near-tie" reading from 2026-08-13 remains a
hypothesis and should not be repeated as a finding.

Frame-level context for the same cell: the port codes 4 distinct uv modes
(DC/V/PAETH/CFL) where C codes 13, over 126 joined blocks with exactly ONE
final-tree flip — so the narrower spread is mostly invisible, and this is the
block where it is not.
